  "result": "The quote now prints 27.06 (it was printing 26.09), and all 6 tests pass.\n\n**Cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9` padded the decimal part on the wrong side. It used `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")`, which turns `\"12.5\"` into `\"12.05\"`, so the price became 1205 cents instead of 1250. The quote came out as 2 \u00d7 12.05 = 24.10 plus 1.99 tax (8.25%) = 26.09.\n\n**Fix:** I changed it to `frac.ljust(2, \"0\")`, so a missing digit is added on the right. The correct total is now 25.00 + 2.06 tax = 27.06. I also checked a few other prices by hand and they convert correctly: `\"12\"` \u2192 1200, `\"12.05\"` \u2192 1205, `\"0.5\"` \u2192 50, `\"12.\"` \u2192 1200.\n\n**Tests:** pytest isn't installed here, so I ran them with `python3 -m unittest`. None of the tests use a one-decimal price, which is why they never caught this. I didn't add one; a test for `to_cents(\"12.5\") == 1250` would be worth adding.",
